     ccpp copies _o_l_d_f_i_l_e to _n_e_w_f_i_l_e, creating _n_e_w_f_i_l_e if necessary.

     In the second form, if the last argument is an existing
     directory, ccpp copies each _f_i_l_e into that directory, keeping
     the same base name.

     The new file's contents are a byte-for-byte copy of the
     source.  Blocks of the source that are entirely zero are
     reproduced by seeking over them in the new file (preserving
     holes in a sparse file) rather than by writing zero bytes,
     except that the last block written always contains real data
     so the copy has the correct length.  The new file's permis-
     sion bits are copied from the source's low 9 mode bits; the
     setuid, setgid, and sticky bits are not copied, and the new
     file is owned by the user running ccpp.

     A file may not be copied onto itself.

