     ccpp prints a message and skips a file if it is a directory,
     cannot be opened or created, is the same file as the des-
     tination, or is a busy shared-text file that cannot be over-
     written; a write error also aborts that file's copy.

NNOOTTEESS
     When copying multiple files into a directory, the exit
     status reflects only whether at least one file was copied
     successfully: ccpp exits 0 if any copy succeeded, and 1 only
     if every copy failed.
